@page "/projects/quiz"

<ProjectPage Title="Real-time quiz app"
             Kicker="Learning project · 2025"
             AccentClass="accent-yellow"
             Summary="A Kahoot-style multiplayer quiz. A host runs the game on a big screen while players join from their phones with a room code, synced live over WebSockets."
             Tech="@(new[] { "JavaScript", "Next.js", "React", "Socket.IO", "MongoDB Atlas", "Prisma", "AWS EC2", "PM2" })"
             Links="@(new[] { new ProjectPage.ProjectLink("Source on GitHub", "https://github.com/Ollie1994/kahoot_clone_v5") })">

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        A rebuild of Kahoot's classroom mode: a host starts a session on a shared screen,
        players join from their own devices using a room code, questions appear for
        everyone at once, and answers come back in real time.
    </p>

    <p>
        It's <strong>deliberately incomplete</strong>, and worth saying so. I built it to
        learn three specific things — Next.js ahead of an internship, how WebSockets handle
        multiple independent groups of users, and deploying something myself while studying
        for the AWS Cloud Practitioner certificate. Once those were covered, polishing it
        further wasn't the point.
    </p>

    <h2>Rooms are the interesting part</h2>

    <p>
        A quiz app is a natural fit for WebSockets, and specifically for <em>rooms</em>.
        Several games have to run at once without leaking into each other: every player
        sees their own game's question at the same moment as everyone else in it, and
        nobody sees anything from another session. The room code is what binds a player's
        socket to the right game.
    </p>

    <p>
        That constraint is what made it a good learning project — request/response wouldn't
        have taught me anything new, whereas keeping many clients in sync with shared
        server state is a genuinely different shape of problem.
    </p>

    <h2>Running it myself</h2>

    <p>
        It's deployed to an <strong>AWS EC2</strong> instance with <strong>PM2</strong>
        keeping the process alive. Provisioning a machine, configuring it and keeping
        something running on it taught me considerably more about what cloud services
        abstract away than the certificate material did on its own.
    </p>

    <p>
        Compared with what I build now, it's the earliest of these projects and the
        simplest — no test suite, no CI, validation left as a "future improvement" in the
        README. I've left it that way rather than quietly going back to fix it.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
